    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Tewodros II</a>'s fortress of Maqdala from boyhood, Menelik escaped in 1865, took the kingdom of Shewa in 1866 and after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv">Yohannes IV</a> was crowned emperor on 3 November 1889.</li><li>His southern conquests, financed partly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, gold and a taxed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a>, Wolayta, Gurage and other peoples into the empire, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> on 1 March 1896 his army defeated Italian colonial forces.</li><li>He is regarded as the architect of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and as a symbol of African resistance to colonialism, while critics among the incorporated peoples, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a>, accuse him of atrocities, land expropriation and forced assimilation.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Menelik was named after the legendary Menelik I, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a>, by his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie">Sahle Selassie</a>, who is said to have prophesied a reign in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> would be reconstituted.</p></aside>
    </section>

