    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1844</span><span class="rf-text">Sahle Maryam, later Menelik II, is born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankober">Ankober</a> in the kingdom of Shewa</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1856</span><span class="rf-text">After fighting between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Tewodros II</a> and his father's Shewans, the boy is captured and taken to Tewodros's court at Maqdala</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1865</span><span class="rf-text">Menelik escapes from Maqdala, and in 1866 proclaims himself Negus of Shewa</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1878</span><span class="rf-text">By the Treaty of Wadara he submits to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv">Yohannes IV</a> with a stone round his neck and is crowned Negus of Shewa with the Emperor's consent</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1887</span><span class="rf-text">Shewan forces defeat the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> at Chelenqo and Harar is annexed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1889</span><span class="rf-text">After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv">Yohannes IV</a> he is crowned emperor on Mount Entoto on 3 November</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1896</span><span class="rf-text">He defeats Italian forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> recognises <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s independence by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-addis-ababa">Treaty of Addis Ababa</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1913</span><span class="rf-text">Menelik dies after years of paralysis</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie.jpg" alt="Sahle Selassie" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie">Sahle Selassie</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Sahla_Sellase_colour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Menelik was born as Sahle Maryam on 19 August 1844 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankober">Ankober</a>, in the kingdom of Shewa, the son of Haile Melekot, Negus of Shewa, and Ejigayehu, said to have been a young woman working for the sovereign's mother Bezabish. His grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie">Sahle Selassie</a> named the child Menelik, after the legendary Menelik I. Haile Melekot at first refused to acknowledge him, but Bezabish had him legitimised by a council of relatives; another version holds that Haile Melekot briefly married the mother. His tutor was Ato Nadew, who stayed close to him all his life.</p><p>In October 1855 fighting broke out between the forces of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Tewodros II</a> and those of Haile Melekot. The Negus ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-berhan">Debre Berhan</a> evacuated and burned, and had loyal Shewan chiefs, among them Darge Sahle Selassie, take Menelik to the Minjar plateau. Darge submitted in February 1856, Tewodros annexed Shewa, and Menelik was taken to the emperor's palace at Maqdala.</p><p>There he was received as a prince. Menelik later said that Tewodros had treated him like a son and told him more than once that he would reign after him. His education was provided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox Church</a>, and he studied military strategy and horsemanship. Tewodros made him a Dejazmach and gave him his daughter Altash in marriage in 1864. He was probably influenced by Tewodros's policies of unification and centralisation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv.jpg" alt="Yohannes IV" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv">Yohannes IV</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yohannes_IV_-_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown but obviously dided 70 years ago, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As Tewodros's authority declined, Menelik escaped from Maqdala on the night of 1 July 1865, aged 21. Tewodros then ordered the execution of the imprisoned son of Queen Worqitu of Wello, who had hoped to exchange Menelik for him. Tewodros's punitive expedition of early 1866 killed mainly civilians, and he ordered the sacking of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a> in November 1866 and a massacre of 800 soldiers around mid-1867; these acts led many soldiers to join Menelik. In August 1866 he reached eastern Shewa and proclaimed himself Negus. At the Battle of Qewet most of the soldiers of the rival Ato Bezabeh joined him, and Bezabeh, first pardoned, was later tried for conspiring with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> and shot.</p><p>In letters of 1867 he called himself King of Kings, but he did not help the British expedition of 1868 against Tewodros, who took his own life. Menelik's imperial ambitions were checked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv">Yohannes IV</a>, crowned emperor in 1872. After Yohannes defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> at Gundet and Gura, Menelik's hopes of replacing him ended. A rebellion in Shewa in 1877, linked to his wife Bafena, further weakened him. In 1878 Yohannes marched on Shewa and, by the Treaty of Wadara of 20 March, Menelik submitted with a stone round his neck and renounced the imperial title, receiving part of Wello and the Emperor's consent to be crowned Negus of Shewa.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa.jpg" alt="Battle of Adwa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Adwa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franko Khoury., PDM-owner</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Confined to Shewa, Menelik turned south. Between 1879 and 1884 his forces moved along the Rift Valley against the Hadiya, Kambata, Silte and Welayta, and he defeated Negus Tekle Haimanot of Gojjam at Embabo in 1882. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Gobana Dache occupied Wollega in 1886, when Menelik moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, and on 6 January 1887 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> was annexed after the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Abd Allah at Chelenqo. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">Ivory</a>, musk, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and gold were exported, and Menelik also benefited from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> conducted by Muslim entrepreneurs but taxed by the Shewan state; the arms bought with these revenues enabled further conquests. Administration was based on garrison towns (katamas) built to house the neftenya. The Swiss engineer Alfred Ilg, who arrived in 1879, became his chief European adviser.</p><p>On 2 May 1889 Menelik signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wuchale">Treaty of Wuchale</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The Italian version gave Italy more power than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a>, and Menelik rejected the treaty on learning of it. In 1895 he called out the nobility, and his army defeated Italian forces at Amba Alagi, took Mekele, and on 1 March 1896 won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a>. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-addis-ababa">Treaty of Addis Ababa</a> Italy recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s independence. Conquests continued to 1900, reaching Lake Rudolf. Modernisation followed: the Menelik thaler of 1894, a postal system, schools, the Bank of Abyssinia (1905), the railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a> and, in 1907, a cabinet of ministers.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Menelik II&#8221; cites where it covers The early phase of his reign, Italo-Ethiopian War, Modernization:</p><ul><li>Bahru Zewde, A History of Modern Ethiopia 1855-1991 (Addis Ababa University Press, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Menelik's opponents within the empire included Ato Bezabeh, Negus Tekle Haimanot of Gojjam and, for years, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv">Yohannes IV</a>. His wife Bafena was implicated in a plot against him in Shewa, and he divorced her in 1882. He sought to be conciliatory toward enemies, and an edict in Shewa forbade religious debate and allowed all religions.</p><p>The expansion was resisted by the peoples it absorbed. The Arsi yielded only in 1886, and the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a> at first refused tribute before being conquered. The campaigns that began in 1894 followed the great famine Kifou Qen, caused by rinderpest, which killed over 90 per cent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. Menelik and his wife Taytu Betul are reported to have owned 70,000 slaves.</p><p>His legacy is contested because of the atrocities committed by his army against civilians and combatants during the annexations, which many historians consider to constitute <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. Awol Allo wrote that Menelik, who masterminded the victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a>, also presided over some of the most brutal atrocities against groups in the south, particularly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a>, and that for Oromos he is "devil incarnate". Critics among the incorporated peoples have also accused him of forced assimilation, land expropriation and cultural suppression, which they say contributed to later ethnic tensions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In mid-1906 Menelik suffered a massive stroke and was seriously ill by January 1907. Fearing the empire might collapse, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> signed a Tripartite Treaty in December 1906 to divide it between themselves in that event. The cabinet of 1907 was intended to answer the same fear. A second stroke came in May 1908 and a third in April 1909 left him paralysed and barely able to speak.</p><p>The succession was followed closely by his wife Taytu, who wished to shift power towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a> and her Yejju family. At the proclamation of the will on 18 May 1909 his grandson Iyasu was named heir presumptive, but Taytu had the will changed so that the heir was named only as Lij ("my child"), which left unclear whether Iyasu or Menelik's daughter Zewditu was meant. On 30 October 1909 the Council of Ministers proclaimed the will with Iyasu's name and appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Bitwoded Tessema Nadew regent. Power remained in Taytu's hands until veterans of the army confronted her in March 1910 and she agreed to stay with Menelik without involving herself in politics. Tessema Nadew held power until he died on 10 April 1911.</p><p>From 1910 to 1913 Menelik's death was announced several times in error. He died on 12 December 1913 and was buried in a mausoleum at the Menelik Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>. Iyasu nominally succeeded him but was never crowned and was deposed by nobles in 1916 in favour of Zewditu, with Ras Tafari Makonnen as regent and heir.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haile-selassie.jpg" alt="Haile Selassie" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haile_Selassie_in_full_dress_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unknown; according to [1] and [2] an official portrait of which b/w copies were distributed by the Ethiopian government, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Menelik is generally considered the founder of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. His borders, set by treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, his ministries, schools, bank and railway, and the extension of the empire to its greatest historical extent shaped the state that followed him. His successors came from his own family: his grandson Iyasu, his daughter Zewditu, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Tafari Makonnen, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> gave him an international reputation. He was praised as a symbol of African resistance to colonialism, and the Adwa Victory Day is celebrated every March in Ethiopia; the victory is also said to have inspired Pan-African movements around the world. A Serb legend, recounted by the anthropologist Mary E. Durham, made Menelik a kinsman of the Montenegrin royal house on the strength of a similarity of names.</p><p>The other side of his memory comes from the peoples of the south. Allo writes that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> are ambivalent toward Adwa because of its association with Menelik. He was a controversial figure for much of his reign.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Menelik II (1844–1913) escaped from captivity under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Tewodros II</a>, became king of Shewa in 1866 and emperor in 1889. His southern conquests extended the empire, his army defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> in 1896, and critics among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> and others charge him with atrocities.</em></p>

